# OPEN predictions, and the STRING LITERAL whose presence in the running binary is what makes each
# one settleable. Format:  F###|marker[,marker...]|the prediction in one line
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# A prediction can only be tested by a binary that CONTAINS the fix it is about. The 1-node unit
# running on engine_build 1785657605 (built 11:00) carries NONE of sink_capped, rules_delivered,
# "WHAT THE SUPERVISOR ALREADY FOUND", "was ALREADY BOUND before", straggler_aborted or "SAME KIND"
# — every one of those was committed after 11:00 and is still held for the next boundary.
#
# So when that run's sink emits no `sink_capped`, THAT IS NOT A FALSIFICATION OF F115. It is an
# UNCONTROLLED ZERO — the standing rule, applied to my own predictions instead of to an arm. The
# trap is live and specific: F116 predicts the next sink takes materially fewer than 25 calls, turn
# count is measurable from any session trace, and it would be entirely natural to read this sink's
# turn count as the test. It is not. The mechanism F116 expects to do the shortening (judge findings
# inherited by the sink) is not in this binary, so ~25 calls here means nothing at all.
#
# `review.py` reads this file and reports, per run, which predictions that run's binary could
# possibly settle. A prediction listed NOT-ON-THIS-BUILD must be left OPEN, never marked failed.
#
# A prediction with NO marker (an outcome measured from events that already exist, e.g. a retry rate
# from task_dispatched.attempt) is written with `-` and is settleable on any build.

